{"name":"Celestial Horizon","hex":"#7c94b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"celestial","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or located in the sky or outer space, where the Sun, Moon, planets, and stars are visible.","Of a nose: upturned, as if towards the sky.\n\n Of or relating to China; Chinese."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Synonym of celestially (“in a celestial manner (adjective senses)”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/celestial"},{"term":"horizon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible horizontal line (in all directions) where the sky appears to meet the earth in the distance.","The range or limit of one's knowledge, experience or interest; a boundary or threshold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horizon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Horizontal coordinate system","blocks":[{"text":"The horizontal coordinate system is a celestial coordinate system that uses the observer's local horizon as the fundamental plane to define two angles of a spherical coordinate system: altitude and azimuth.\nTherefore, the horizontal coordinate system is sometimes called the az/el system, the alt/az system, or the alt-azimuth system, among others. In an altazimuth mount of a telescope, the instrument's two axes follow altitude and azimuth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Azimuth-Altitude_schematic.svg/330px-Azimuth-Altitude_schematic.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizontal_coordinate_system","wikidata":"Q625696"},{"title":"Horizon","blocks":[{"text":"Most commonly, the horizon is the border between the surface of a celestial body and its sky when viewed from the perspective of an observer on or above the surface of the celestial body. This concept is further refined as:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Horizons.svg/330px-Horizons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon","wikidata":"Q43261"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/500px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/1280px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/500px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The curvature of Earth's horizon as observed from a Space Shuttle at an altitude of 226 km (140 mi).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth's_horizon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg/500px-Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg/1280px-Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Riv